//? Title: Kernel PCA: Centring, Nonlinear Features and New Observations
//? Subtitle: Exact small kernels, spectral limits and an iris projection with frozen training geometry
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A curved collection of observations may need more than a rotation of its original
measurement axes to reveal useful low-dimensional structure. We want coordinates that
retain variation in a chosen nonlinear feature space, together with evidence of what
those coordinates discard. Kernel PCA obtains them from a centred matrix of pairwise
kernel similarities. Three-point linear and Gaussian calculations and a four-point
quadratic feature map make centring, normalization and rank explicit. Eight symmetric
ring points show that radial information need not be the leading component at every
scale. Iris then illustrates kernel sensitivity without choosing parameters from species
labels, followed by projection of untouched observations using a training-only centre.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Choose the geometry before seeking principal axes

For raw numeric predictors \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\), a positive semidefinite kernel
represents inner products \(k(x_i,x_j)=\langle\phi(x_i),\phi(x_j)\rangle\).
A linear kernel keeps the original geometry; a nonlinear kernel changes it before
performing ordinary principal-component analysis in feature space. Here the choices are

$$
 k_L(x,z)=x^Tz,\qquad k_R(x,z)=\exp(-\gamma\|x-z\|^2),\qquad
 k_P(x,z)=(\gamma x^Tz+c)^d.
$$
\alttext(Linear similarity is the dot product. Gaussian similarity decays with squared distance. The polynomial kernel raises a scaled dot product plus an offset to an integer power.)

The RBF parameter gamma is an inverse squared length scale, with
\(\ell=(2\gamma)^{-1/2}\). Polynomial d is a positive integer, gamma is positive
and c is nonnegative, giving a PSD kernel on arbitrary real vectors. Fractional powers
and negative offsets are rejected rather than treated as valid feature maps by default.
All active parameters and retained predictors must be finite; unrepresentable kernel
values are rejected.

`KernelPCA` uses predictors in their supplied units. It does not standardize them or
select gamma. Uniformly rescaling x by a requires rescaling RBF gamma by
\(1/a^2\) to preserve similarities. Changing only one column's units changes the metric
in a way one scalar gamma cannot generally undo. In a train/test analysis, any external
scaling must also be learned on training rows and frozen for the test rows.

>> Centre features, not the original measurements alone

Let \(K_{ij}=k(x_i,x_j)\), \(\mu=n^{-1}\sum_i\phi(x_i)\) and
\(H=I-11^T/n\). The Gram matrix of centred feature vectors is

$$
 K_c=HKH,\qquad (K_c)_{ij}=K_{ij}-\bar K_{i\cdot}-\bar K_{\cdot j}+\bar K_{\cdot\cdot}.
$$
\alttext(Double centring subtracts each kernel row and column mean and adds the grand mean. The resulting matrix is the inner-product matrix of feature vectors centred on their training mean.)

Thus \(K_c1=0\), its rank is at most n−1, and an unchanged constant kernel has zero
centred rank. Centering raw x suffices for a linear kernel, but does not generally centre
nonlinear features. In the quadratic example below, the mean of x is zero while the
mean of its squared feature is 2.5.

For numerical stability the linear implementation forms centred input dot products.
For RBF it centres `expm1(-gamma*distance_squared)` before restoring any common
constant: the added all-ones matrix vanishes under H. This retains a tiny-gamma signal
even when every entry in a double-precision raw kernel has rounded to one.
`kernel_matrix()` exposes rounded raw K; `centered_kernel_matrix()` exposes the more
accurately computed centred geometry.

>> Normalize eigenvectors to obtain scores and feature-space variance

For \(K_cv_j=\lambda_jv_j\), \(\|v_j\|=1\) and positive lambda, a unit feature
axis and its training scores are

$$
 u_j=\frac{\sum_i v_{ij}(\phi(x_i)-\mu)}{\sqrt{\lambda_j}},\qquad
 Z_{ij}=\langle\phi(x_i)-\mu,u_j\rangle=\sqrt{\lambda_j}v_{ij}.
$$
\alttext(A kernel eigenvector divided by the square root of its eigenvalue gives the dual coefficients of a unit feature-space axis. Training scores multiply that eigenvector by the square root instead.)

Consequently \(Z^TZ=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_q)\).
The Gram eigenvalue is the sum of squared scores; its sample covariance variance is
\(\lambda_j/(n-1)\). These are feature-space variances, with no general interpretation
as centimetres squared of the original measurements. `eigenvalues()` preserves the
retained Gram-scale API; `all_eigenvalues()` and `spectrum()` expose the full signed
spectrum and the sample-variance normalization.

Signs are arbitrary; the implementation fixes a largest-magnitude eigenvector entry
positive. Repeated eigenvalues permit rotations of an entire eigenspace, so comparisons
should use projectors or score Gram matrices, not signed columns. Truncating through a
tied block can make even the selected subspace ambiguous. Numerical rank uses
\(64n\epsilon\lambda_{\max}\); requests beyond it throw rather than promoting
roundoff into a component. Materially negative eigenvalues also throw.

For a PSD kernel, q leading axes minimize total training feature-space projection loss:

$$
 \sum_i\|\phi(x_i)-\mu-\Pi_q(\phi(x_i)-\mu)\|^2
 =\operatorname{tr}(K_c)-\sum_{j\le q}\lambda_j
 =\sum_{j>q}\lambda_j.
$$
\alttext(The total squared feature-space projection loss is the sum of discarded positive eigenvalues. Keeping leading axes minimizes this loss among subspaces of the same dimension.)

`spectrum()` reports the tail energy and cumulative share within the chosen kernel.
The numerical summary uses resolved eigenvalues above tolerance. A larger retained share
across different kernels is not proof of a better map: those kernels define different
feature spaces. Low-dimensional coordinates need not preserve original distances,
classes or manifold geodesics. Nor is a feature-space projection an inverse transform
back to raw measurements; this implementation does not solve a pre-image problem.

>> Project new observations using the training mean

For a new row x, centre its cross-kernel row against the frozen training frame:

$$
 k_c(x,i)=k(x,x_i)-\frac1n\sum_jk(x,x_j)-\bar K_{\cdot i}+\bar K_{\cdot\cdot},
 \qquad z_j(x)=\sum_i k_c(x,i)\frac{v_{ij}}{\sqrt{\lambda_j}}.
$$
\alttext(A new kernel row subtracts its own mean over training points and the stored training column means, then adds the training grand mean. Its dot product with normalized dual coefficients gives the new scores.)

The query-row mean is over training landmarks, not other test observations. Adding
queries to a batch cannot change an existing row's coordinates. `transform()` uses this
rule; refitting on the combined frame, or separately centring a test kernel matrix,
would answer different questions.

`projection_details()` additionally evaluates
\(\|\phi(x)-\mu\|^2=k(x,x)-2\overline{k(x,X)}+\bar K_{\cdot\cdot}\)
and subtracts the sum of retained squared scores. The remainder is a feature-space
projection loss, not a predictive test error or calibrated anomaly score. Missing or
nonfinite query predictors retain their row positions with NaN scores and a status;
training nulls are omitted jointly and original positions remain in `kept_row_indices()`.

Dense fitting costs \(O(n^2p+n^3)\) time and \(O(n^2+np)\) storage. Projecting m new
rows costs \(O(mn(p+q))\). The eigensolver is scaled before decomposition, so a small
kernel scale is not confused with zero by an absolute stopping threshold.

> Simple examples

>> Three linear points expose centring and out-of-sample projection

For x = (0,1,2), the raw Gram matrix has rows (0,0,0), (0,1,2), (0,2,4).
The training mean is one, so the centred matrix has rows (1,0,−1), (0,0,0),
(−1,0,1). Its only positive eigenvalue is two, scores are (−1,0,1) up to one
common sign, and sample variance is one. A new x=3 projects to magnitude two on
the same axis; it is not centred by itself to zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kpca_linear_three.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1,2});
  const dm::stats::KernelPCA m(d,{"x"},{.n_components=1,.kernel="linear"});dm::dstruct::DataFrame q;q.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{3});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame matrices;for(std::size_t j=0;j<3;++j){matrices.add_column("K"+std::to_string(j),m.kernel_matrix().col(j));matrices.add_column("Kc"+std::to_string(j),m.centered_kernel_matrix().col(j));}
  auto scores=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),row=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();scores.points({0,1,2},m.dimension(0),"training scores",{37,99,235},6).points({3},m.transform(q).col(0),"new observation",{220,38,38},6).title("One axis with the training centre").x_label("raw x").y_label("kernel score").y_limits(-3,3);
  row.bars({0,1,2},m.centered_kernel_matrix().row(0),"Kc first row",{37,99,235},.5).title("Centred inner products against x = 0").x_label("training row").y_label("centred similarity").y_limits(-1.5,1.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(scores).add(row).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kpca_linear_three.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\nNew x = 3:\n\n{}\n",matrices.to_md(3),m.spectrum().to_md(6),m.projection_details(q).to_md(6));
}
```
