> Checking the theory

>> Small gamma approaches scaled linear PCA; large gamma loses a preferred axis

Expanding the RBF kernel at small positive gamma gives
\(K_{ij}=1-\gamma\|x_i-x_j\|^2+O(\gamma^2)\), hence
\(K_c=2\gamma X_cX_c^T+O(\gamma^2)\).
Leading eigenspaces approach linear PCA when separated by an eigengap; score magnitudes
shrink as \(\sqrt{2\gamma}\). They do not approach raw linear scores without that
rescaling. In the three-point example, the odd eigenvalue is approximately 4 gamma and
the even one approximately \(2\gamma^2\); the tiny second mode eventually falls below
numerical rank tolerance and is not requested in the sweep.

For distinct points, gamma tending to infinity gives \(K\to I\) and \(K_c\to H\),
with n−1 equal eigenvalues of one. It is H, not I, after centring. There is then no
preferred ordering of directions, and a two-dimensional truncation captures only
\(2/(n-1)\) of feature energy. Duplicates change this limit to blocks of identical
kernel rows. A displayed direction at a nearly repeated eigenvalue should not be given
a stable scientific interpretation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kpca_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-1,0,1});dm::dstruct::DataFrame results;std::vector<double> logg,ratio,share;
  for(double g:{1e-100,1e-10,1e-5,.001,.01,.1,1.,10.,100.}){const dm::stats::KernelPCA m(d,{"x"},{.n_components=1,.gamma=g});auto row=m.diagnostics();row.add_column("gamma",std::vector<double>{g});row.add_column("lambda_over_4gamma",std::vector<double>{m.eigenvalues()[0]/(4*g)});row.add_column("lambda1",std::vector<double>{m.eigenvalues()[0]});results=results.nrows()?results.concat_rows(row):row;if(g>=1e-5){logg.push_back(std::log10(g));ratio.push_back(m.eigenvalues()[0]/(4*g));share.push_back(m.diagnostics().double_at("retained_share",0));}}
  auto limit=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),energy=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();limit.lines(logg,ratio,"lambda1 / (4 gamma)",{37,99,235}).title("The scaled linear limit").x_label("log10 gamma").y_label("eigenvalue ratio").y_limits(0,1.3);energy.lines(logg,share,"one-axis energy share",{37,99,235}).title("At large gamma the two modes tie").x_label("log10 gamma").y_label("retained feature energy").y_limits(0,1.3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(limit).add(energy).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kpca_limits.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",results.select({"gamma","lambda1","lambda_over_4gamma","rank","retained_share","relative_eigen_residual"}).to_md(dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions{.digits=6,.scientific={"gamma","lambda1","relative_eigen_residual"}}));
}
```
