\caption(Gamma changes both the overall feature scale and the distribution of feature energy. The extremely small gamma row exercises stable centring even when raw similarities round to one.)
\alttext(Log-gamma curves show the leading eigenvalue divided by four gamma approaching one at small gamma, and one-axis energy share approaching one half at large gamma. A table includes very small parameter values and numerical rank.)


>> Eight ring points distinguish radial information from variance ordering

Use four axis-aligned points on radius one and four on radius three, with no noise or
randomness. The ring contrast is an exact eigenvector by rotational symmetry, while
angular modes occur in repeated pairs. At gamma 0.01 the leading pair is angular; at
0.3 the radial contrast becomes the leading mode. The input table and embedding plots below
use ring labels only for colour. This controlled example does not estimate classifier
accuracy, and a leading radial axis is not guaranteed for other radii, sample patterns or
gamma values. Rotations within the repeated angular pair are immaterial to its subspace.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kpca_eight_rings.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{1,0,-1,0,3,0,-3,0});d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,1,0,-1,0,3,0,-3});const std::vector<std::string> labels={"inner","inner","inner","inner","outer","outer","outer","outer"};
  const dm::stats::KernelPCA angular(d,{"x","y"},{.gamma=.01}),radial(d,{"x","y"},{.gamma=.3});auto a=angular.plot_embedding(labels).title("Small gamma: leading angular pair").y_limits(-.5,.7),b=radial.plot_embedding(labels).title("Gamma 0.3: leading radial contrast").y_limits(-.8,1.2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kpca_eight_rings.svg");
  std::print("Input points:\n\n{}\n\nGamma .01:\n\n{}\n\nGamma .3:\n\n{}\n",d.to_md(1),angular.spectrum().head(5).to_md(6),radial.spectrum().head(5).to_md(6));
}
```
