| 0.0100 | 80 | 12.409033 | 0.953140 | 0.003877 | 0.982572 | 0.989532 |
| 0.0500 | 126 | 45.087952 | 0.909603 | 0.027172 | 0.964501 | 0.968600 |
| 0.1300 | 147 | 74.298432 | 0.834523 | 0.081965 | 0.940377 | 0.962320 |
| 0.5000 | 148 | 107.234426 | 0.582306 | 0.298608 | 0.936074 | 0.962235 |
| 2.0000 | 148 | 131.531448 | 0.280574 | 0.630848 | 0.893437 | 0.932991 |
| 10.0000 | 148 | 145.620013 | 0.097499 | 0.876148 | 0.787450 | 0.769824 |
| 100.0000 | 148 | 148.890909 | 0.024315 | 0.968471 | 0.615430 | 0.575385 |

\image(figures/kpca_iris_gamma.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The scan measures kernel compression and raw-metric neighbourhood preservation on the same 150 observations; it is a descriptive parameter study without label-based tuning.)
\alttext(One panel shows two-component retained feature energy against log gamma, and the other shows neighbourhood trustworthiness and continuity. The table includes rank, feature energy and projection loss for every declared gamma.)


At gamma 0.0001, the two-axis retained share is 0.977376 and raw-metric
trustworthiness is 0.982979. At gamma 10 they are 0.097499 and 0.787450. These
changes show why a smooth-looking two-axis map needs a scale-specific interpretation.
The data include a repeated four-measurement tuple: high-gamma rank is 148 rather
than the distinct-point upper bound 149. Numerical rank at smaller gamma also reflects
the declared relative tolerance, rather than estimating an intrinsic manifold dimension.

>> Fit on 120 flowers, project the remaining 30

Use the prespecified `train_test_split(iris,.2,42,"Species")`, with ten test flowers per
species, and fix gamma at 0.13 before this split analysis. Only the 120 training flowers
define feature centring, the eigensystem and dual coefficients. Species is used to balance
the split, not as a predictor; the frozen transform is unsupervised conditional on that
sampling design. Projecting the 30 held-out rows does not refit any of these quantities.

Training and test projection losses share the same feature-space definition. Their
comparison checks how this fixed subspace represents other flowers on one small split;
it is not regression accuracy, classification accuracy or a claim that a different
kernel would generalize better. The historical data also do not define a representative
population sampling experiment.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kpca_iris_projection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),.2,42,"Species");const std::vector<std::string> f={"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};const dm::stats::KernelPCA m(train,f,{.gamma=.13});const auto projected=m.transform(test);
  auto scores=m.plot_embedding().title("Training axes and new projections");scores.points(projected.col(0),projected.col(1),"30 test projections",{220,38,38},5).y_limits(-.9,1.2);
  auto a=m.projection_details(train),b=m.projection_details(test);a.add_column("sample",std::vector<std::string>(train.nrows(),"training"));b.add_column("sample",std::vector<std::string>(test.nrows(),"test"));const auto summary=a.concat_rows(b)|dm::group_by("sample")|dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("centered_norm_squared","mean_centered_norm_squared"),dm::agg::mean("residual_energy","mean_projection_loss"));
  auto loss=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();loss.points(a.numeric_column("centered_norm_squared").values(),a.numeric_column("residual_energy").values(),"training",{37,99,235}).points(b.numeric_column("centered_norm_squared").values(),b.numeric_column("residual_energy").values(),"test",{220,38,38}).title("Loss in the frozen RBF feature space").x_label("squared norm about training centre").y_label("squared orthogonal projection loss").y_limits(0,1.2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(scores).add(loss).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kpca_iris_projection.svg");
  std::print("{} training, {} test flowers.\n\n{}\n\nFirst five test projections:\n\n{}\n",train.nrows(),test.nrows(),summary|dm::to_md(6),b.head(5).select({"query_row","centered_norm_squared","projected_norm_squared","residual_energy","status"}).to_md(6));
}
```
