\caption(Out-of-sample projection uses the training kernel means and axes. Test feature-space loss measures representation by that fixed subspace, without refitting or a pre-image calculation.)
\alttext(Training and projected test coordinates share one pair of training axes. A second panel compares feature-space projection loss with centred squared norm for both samples; tables summarize their losses and individual query diagnostics.)


The mean training projection loss is 0.082928 and the mean held-out loss is 0.080447
in this fixed RBF feature space. Similar averages on this split do not establish a
population guarantee; individual held-out projection diagnostics remain visible.

> Conclusion

Kernel PCA performs ordinary variance maximization after choosing and centring a feature
geometry. The exact examples distinguish raw and centred kernels, Gram eigenvalues and
sample variances, and nonlinear structure from the ordering of principal axes. The RBF
limits explain both scaled linear behaviour at small gamma and the loss of preferred
axes at large gamma. Iris confirms the linear-PCA identity and exposes the dependence of
compression and neighbourhood preservation on kernel scale. New observations use training
centring and normalized dual coefficients; feature-space projection losses remain
representation diagnostics rather than original-unit prediction errors.

> References

- Schölkopf, B., Smola, A. and Müller, K.-R. (1998). [Nonlinear Component Analysis as a Kernel Eigenvalue Problem|https://doi.org/10.1162/089976698300017467]. ~Neural Computation~, 10(5), 1299–1319. Feature-space centring, normalization and kernel projections.
- scikit-learn developers. [KernelPCA|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.decomposition.KernelPCA.html]. Canonical implementation reference for positive kernel eigenpairs and frozen-training transforms; parameter and rank conventions must be matched when comparing.
- Venna, J. and Kaski, S. (2001). [Neighborhood Preservation in Nonlinear Projection Methods: An Experimental Study|https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-44668-0_68]. ~ICANN 2001~, LNCS 2130, 485–491. Rank-based neighbourhood diagnostics.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. ~Annals of Eugenics~, 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurements.
